Synthesize the following 3 reviews into a meta-review:

- {"summary": "The paper proposes CDGS, a compositional diffusion sampler that embeds population-based guided search within denoising to avoid mode averaging when composing multi-modal local models for long-horizon planning. It combines iterative resampling for message passing and a pruning/ranking metric via DDIM inversion curvature. Qualitative and some comparative results are shown across robotics (OGBench, TAMP), panoramas, and long video generation.", "significance_and_novelty": "Addresses a known failure mode (mode averaging) in compositional diffusion by integrating cross-entropy-like guided search and iterative resampling, plus a DDIM-inversion-based pruning heuristic, and demonstrates cross-domain applicability.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Sec. 3, Alg. 1\u20132, and Fig. 3\u20134 clearly identify mode averaging in compositional sampling and propose a concrete, general inference-time remedy via population search, iterative resampling, and pruning.", "Sec. 4\u20135 and Fig. 5\u20137 demonstrate broad applicability (robotic planning, panoramas, long video) with consistent qualitative improvements in global coherence/local feasibility.", "Fig. 5(c\u2013d) provides useful evidence that success increases with batch size and resampling steps, suggesting a practical adaptive-compute knob for longer horizons."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Equations and exact formulations referenced as Eq. 1/3/5 in Sec. 2\u20133 are not visible in the provided text, preventing verification of the composed score, Bethe approximation details, and the ranking objective.", "Tables 1\u20135 in Sec. 4\u20135 are not fully legible in the provided excerpt (many entries missing), so key claims such as \u201cmatches oracle performance on seven robot manipulation tasks\u201d (Abstract) and \u201cmatches SD\u201d (Table 4) cannot be quantitatively verified.", "The pruning metric J based on DDIM inversion curvature (Sec. 3.1) is heuristic and its connection to likelihood is not theoretically justified here; ablations isolating pruning vs. resampling across domains are limited and notation for w/o PR/RP is ambiguous (Sec. 4\u20135).", "Compute fairness is unclear: CDGS relies on large batch B and resampling steps U (Fig. 5), but wall-clock costs and matched test-time compute vs. baselines (OGBench, TAMP, panorama/video) are not reported in the provided text."], "suggestions": ["Sec. 3.2: Explore alternative message passing (e.g., learned attention across overlaps) to compare with iterative resampling as the horizon grows.", "Sec. 5: Complement automatic metrics with small-scale human preference studies for panoramas and long videos to assess coherence trade-offs.", "Release adapters and example configs for additional base models to ease adoption beyond SD-2.0 and CogVideoX-2B."], "rating": 5, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 2, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper proposes Compositional Diffusion with Guided Search (CDGS), which embeds population-based search, iterative resampling, and likelihood-based pruning inside diffusion denoising to avoid mode averaging when composing short-horizon generative models into coherent long-horizon plans. CDGS is evaluated on OGBench robotic planning/TAMP, panoramas, and long video generation, generally matching or outperforming baselines and showing scaling with inference-time compute.", "significance_and_novelty": "Addresses a salient failure mode (mode averaging) in compositional diffusion by integrating guided search and pruning into denoising, and demonstrates cross-domain applicability. Novelty over verifier-guided search and prior compositional methods is plausible but somewhat incremental.", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Sec. 3 and 3.2: Clear algorithmic contribution integrating population search, iterative resampling, and DDIM-inversion\u2013based pruning into the denoising loop, yielding a plug-and-play sampler.", "Sec. 4: Broad and convincing empirical results on OGBench Maze/Scene and TAMP, often outperforming compositional baselines (GSC, CompDiffuser) and competitive with privileged methods; includes ablations showing B and U are both necessary.", "Sec. 5: Cross-domain generalization to panoramas and long videos, outperforming overlap score-averaging (MD) and matching SD without explicit perceptual loss (Tab. 4, Fig. 6).", "Sec. 4 (Right): Scaling analysis demonstrates adaptive inference-time compute benefits, aligning with the method\u2019s design."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Alg. 2/App. D (referenced in Sec. 3): The curvature-based \"likelihood\" surrogate via DDIM inversion lacks a rigorous justification or quantitative calibration; we could not verify its reliability from the provided text.", "Sec. 5: Quantitative evaluation details for panoramas/videos (e.g., full metrics beyond CLIP/LPIPS, FVD or human studies) are limited in the excerpts; claims of matching SD are hard to assess without seeing Tab. 4 numbers.", "Sec. 4: Baseline fairness is partly unclear since some results are borrowed from prior papers; details of reimplementation settings and oracle planning horizon usage could bias comparisons.", "Sec. 6: Novelty relative to verifier-guided diffusion search and compositional methods (GSC, Diffusion-CCSP, CompDiffuser) is not sharply delineated; contribution may be incremental."], "suggestions": ["Sec. 3.2: Clarify the probabilistic factorization used in iterative resampling with a short derivation and notation glossary for overlaps and dependencies.", "App. D/Alg. 2: Include a simple 1D/2D toy example visualizing the curvature-based pruning signal versus true likelihood to build intuition."], "rating": 6, "confidence": 3, "soundness": 3, "presentation": 3, "contribution": 3}

- {"summary": "The paper proposes Compositional Diffusion with Guided Search (CDGS), an inference-time algorithm that combats mode-averaging in compositional diffusion by embedding population-based search into denoising, adding iterative resampling across overlapping segments, and pruning via a likelihood-based ranking. Claims include oracle-level performance on seven manipulation tasks and coherent panorama/video generation.", "significance_and_novelty": "Addresses an important failure mode of compositional diffusion\u2014mode averaging\u2014by coupling search with score-based composition and message passing. The integration of population-based sampling, overlap resampling, and pruning within denoising appears novel and broadly applicable across robotics and\u89c6\u89c9", "reasons_for_acceptance": ["Section 3 and Algorithms 1\u20132 present a clear guided-search procedure integrated into diffusion denoising that directly targets multimodality in compositional settings.", "Section 3.2\u2019s iterative resampling over overlaps provides a principled local-to-global information flow mechanism to promote global coherence.", "Figure 2 and the Introduction motivate broad applicability (robotic planning, panoramas, videos) with a plug-and-play sampler design.", "The use of population-based sampling with elite selection (Algorithm 1) is a practical design likely to improve exploration over local modes."], "reasons_for_rejection": ["Abstract and Introduction claim matching oracle performance on seven robot tasks and strong panorama/video results, but no quantitative evidence, baselines, or protocols are available in the provided content to verify these claims.", "Section 2\u2019s approximation for overlapping-variable scores via averaging conditionals (Eq. 3 reference) lacks derivation and conditions of validity, weakening theoretical justification.", "Method details for the pruning objective J(\u00b7) (Eq. 5) and the likelihood used for ranking are referenced but not defined in the provided text, preventing assessment of soundness.", "The computational cost of population-based search with iterative resampling at every diffusion step (Algorithms 1\u20132) is not reported in the retrieved sections, leaving scalability to long horizons and high-res media unclear."], "suggestions": [], "rating": 5, "confidence": 2, "soundness": 2, "presentation": 2, "contribution": 3}
